<section id="rigor" aria-labelledby="h-rigor">
  <h2 id="h-rigor" class="sec">How we measure</h2>
  <p class="sub">The rules behind every score on this page.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Fixed task sets.</b> We develop on 24 practice tasks. The other 147 stay unseen until the code is frozen.</li>
    <li><b>The AI plays every move.</b> A language model (<code>gpt-4.1-mini</code>) watches the video and picks each action. Final scores never replay recorded moves.</li>
    <li><b>The benchmark's own judge.</b> Gemini 3.1 Pro scores each video with the benchmark's settings, and is never told which model made it.</li>
    <li><b>Score twice.</b> A final score averages two or more scorings and reports how much they disagree.</li>
    <li><b>Ignore small differences.</b> A gain smaller than twice that disagreement does not count.</li>
    <li><b>Everything is logged.</b> Each run is a row in a public file with its exact code version. This page reads it live.</li>
  </ol>
  <details class="rules-wrap"><summary>Technical detail: the 8 rules and where the code enforces each</summary>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:10px">
    <table class="rules">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Why</th><th scope="col">Where it lives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Iterate on dev, freeze before heldout</td><td>Tuning on the tasks you report overfits the benchmark.</td><td class="enf"><code>splits/make_splits.py</code> (seed 20261114) fixes the split; scoring heldout only after freeze is a team rule <span class="badge warn">convention, not code</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Final numbers are closed loop</td><td>Replaying recorded actions removes the agent, the hardest part of interactive play.</td><td class="enf"><code>--action-source agent|refined</code>; dataset rows badged "open loop, A/B only"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Silent open-loop fallback is caught</td><td>Upstream falls back to dataset or WAIT actions on agent API errors and still exits 0.</td><td class="enf"><code>pwa gen</code> checks every mp4 and counts <code>[agent] fallback</code> lines</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Every run gets a new name</td><td>Upstream <code>--skip-done</code> silently reuses old videos.</td><td class="enf"><code>pwa gen</code> refuses an existing run directory unless <code>--resume</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Verifier settings are untouched</td><td>Comparable to the paper's leaderboard only at the same judge settings.</td><td class="enf"><code>pwa score</code> runs the upstream verifier from the pinned submodule; only the IF third-person gate is re-implemented, because upstream crashes on it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Missing scores are visible</td><td>Dropping a failed rollout can quietly raise the mean.</td><td class="enf"><code>pwa report</code> logs missing videos (<code>n_missing</code>) and unscored rollouts (<code>n_dropped</code>); <code>--strict</code> scores both as 1.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gains must clear the noise</td><td>Verifier passes disagree; a single lucky pass is not an improvement.</td><td class="enf"><code>pwa compare</code> and the <a href="#progress">Progress</a> table use 2x pass std</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Branch code is flagged</td><td>A score should describe code anyone can check out from main.</td><td class="enf">Workflow records <code>branch</code>; non-main rows badged "unmerged code"</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  </details>
</section>
